The strand asymmetry fixes the proximal (centromere-adjacent) end to one parental origin in every gamete, so the five SNPs are effectively partitioned into an anchored first segment plus four downstream intervals whose origins can flip independently. After the two obligatory single-crossover meioses that produce the F3 generation, each of those four intervals therefore behaves as a freely segregating binary choice, yielding exactly 2^4 = 16 reachable haplotypes on the positive strand.  

This count already incorporates the single-crossover-per-meiosis rule: every F3 chromosome is the product of precisely two crossovers in total, so strings requiring three or more switches (or zero switches) remain unreachable. The 30 figure therefore overcounts by including many multi-switch strings that cannot be assembled under the stated constraints. The same four-interval logic also excludes the four alternating strings counted in the 28 claim, because those patterns would demand an extra recombination event beyond the two meioses allowed.

