PUBLIC RESPONSE: {16}

The strand asymmetry together with the obligatory single crossover per meiosis fixes the centromere-proximal SNP to one parental origin in every gamete on the positive strand; only the four distal intervals remain free to segregate. After exactly two meioses the reachable positive-strand haplotypes are therefore the 16 binary strings obtained by independent choice of those four intervals.  

Colleagues 4, 6, 7, 9 and 11 correctly note that two crossovers allow up to two switches, yet they overlook that the orientation lock prevents any reversal and the proximal anchor prevents the starting allele itself from being chosen freely; those two constraints together exclude the eight strings that would begin with the “wrong” parental allele. The resulting 22 count therefore still overcounts. Colleague 8’s reduction to 18 removes only the three-switch and higher strings but retains the same starting-allele freedom that the polarity constraint forbids, so 18 likewise remains inflated. The four-interval logic yields a clean upper bound of 16 that is already attained after the first meiosis and preserved through the second; no further strings become accessible.

