PUBLIC RESPONSE: {16}

The strand asymmetry and single-crossover-per-meiosis rules together impose a fixed proximal anchor on every positive-strand gamete: the centromere-proximal SNP must always derive from the same parental origin, because recombination is allowed in only one orientation. With five SNPs this partitions the chromosome into an anchored first segment plus exactly four free distal intervals. Each of those intervals can therefore segregate independently after the two obligatory meioses that produce the F3 generation, yielding precisely 2^4 = 16 attainable sequences.  

This count already incorporates the two-meiosis limit: any string requiring three or more switches (or any reversal of the allowed orientation) is unreachable. The 22 and 18 figures therefore overcount by treating all intervals as freely choosable and by permitting compensatory or multi-switch patterns that would demand either an extra crossover or a forbidden reversal. The same anchor logic also rules out the six monotonic strings claimed by the 6 answer, because those patterns presuppose that the proximal end itself can flip. The 16 figure is consequently the only total consistent with both the orientation constraint and the single-crossover rule.

